<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10691v1/mnist_non_acc_eps_delta.png" id="S5.F5.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:157/95;" width="157" height="95" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((d)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10691v1/isolet_non_acc_eps_delta.png" id="S5.F5.sf5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:157/95;" width="157" height="95" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((e)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F5.sf6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10691v1/ucihar_non_acc_eps_delta.png" id="S5.F5.sf6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:157/95;" width="157" height="95" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((f)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Model accuracy trends under varying privacy budget (<math id="S5.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>) and privacy loss coefficient (<math id="S5.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) values for IID and non-IID scenarios. (a)–(c) correspond to IID distributions for MNIST, ISOLET, and UCI-HAR datasets, respectively. (d)–(f) show the results under non-IID distributions for the same datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10691v1/IID_nclients_acc.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/117;" width="238" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Three curves show MNIST, ISOLET, and UCI-HAR accuracy across communication rounds, with each round’s marker giving the mean over <math id="S5.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\{10,\ldots,1000\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\{10,\ldots,1000\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the shaded band indicating <math id="S5.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> standard deviation across those <math id="S5.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> values.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> extends the analysis by reporting accuracy as a function of the number of clients under IID partitioning for MNIST, ISOLET, and UCI-HAR across communication rounds. We vary <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> while keeping the total training set fixed and evenly distributed, so any change in performance reflects the effect of partitioning and coordination rather than data volume. For each round, the plot summarizes the mean accuracy across client counts together with the standard deviation. The variability is small for MNIST, remaining below <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>, moderate for ISOLET, reaching up to <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.31\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.31</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.31\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and larger for UCI-HAR, reaching up to <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.37\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.37</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.37\%</annotation></semantics></math>. The standard deviation is nearly constant from round to round within each dataset, indicating that uncertainty is driven primarily by the dataset and task rather than the communication round.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10691v1/IID_Nsamples_D_acc_mnist.png" id="S5.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:157/140;" width="157" height="140" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10691v1/IID_Nsamples_D_acc_isolet.png" id="S5.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:157/140;" width="157" height="140" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F7.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10691v1/IID_Nsamples_D_acc_ucihar.png" id="S5.F7.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:157/140;" width="157" height="140" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Impact of the number of training samples per client and hypervector size on model performance under IID settings. Subfigures (a)–(c) show classification accuracy for MNIST, ISOLET, and UCI-HAR datasets, respectively.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Complementing the client-count study in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> presents the effect of hypervector dimensionality and the number of training samples per client on model accuracy. Figs. <a href="#S5.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a>, <a href="#S5.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, and <a href="#S5.F7.sf3" title="In Figure 7 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(c)</span></a> correspond to the MNIST, ISOLET, and UCI-HAR datasets, respectively, with hypervector sizes varied from 50 to 500 and training samples per client from 50 to 250 for MNIST, and from 5 to 25 for ISOLET and UCI-HAR.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For the MNIST dataset, accuracy depends strongly on hypervector size. For instance, with 200 training samples, accuracy improves steadily from <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="65.35\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>65.35</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">65.35\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="91.86\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>91.86</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">91.86\%</annotation></semantics></math> as hypervector size increases from 50 to 500. Conversely, when hypervector size is fixed at 300, accuracy increases more gradually from <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86.68\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>86.68</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86.68\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="92.27\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>92.27</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">92.27\%</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of training samples grows from 50 to 250. This indicates that hypervector dimensionality has a stronger influence on MNIST performance than the number of samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For ISOLET, the trend is reversed: accuracy is more sensitive to the number of training samples than to hypervector size. For example, when the hypervector size is fixed at 500, accuracy improves from <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="53.56\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>53.56</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">53.56\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.99\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>77.99</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.99\%</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of training samples per client increases from 5 to 25. However, for a fixed training size, enlarging the hypervector dimension has only a marginal effect. At 5 training samples per client, accuracy increases by <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.70\%</annotation></semantics></math> when hypervector size increases from 50 to 500, and at 25 training samples the increase is <math id="S5.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.53\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.53</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.53\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The UCI-HAR dataset follows the same pattern as ISOLET, where accuracy is more dependent on training data size than on hypervector dimensionality. With hypervector size fixed at 50, accuracy increases by <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.04\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.04</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.04\%</annotation></semantics></math> as training samples per client grow from 5 to 25. When the hypervector size is 500, the increase is <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.86\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.86</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.86\%</annotation></semantics></math>. However, once clients already have 25 samples each, further increasing hypervector dimensionality yields only a modest <math id="S5.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.05\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.05</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.05\%</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy increase. These results highlight that for ISOLET and UCI-HAR, additional data availability is more beneficial than expanding hypervector size.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10691v1/nonIID_trainsamples_acc.svg" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/118;" width="238" height="118"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Accuracy of PrivateDFL over communication rounds for different portions of training samples on the non-IID datasets.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Extending to the non-IID scenario, Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> reports accuracy across communication rounds while varying the number of training samples per client. For MNIST with 50 samples per client, accuracy starts at <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.68\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.68</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.68\%</annotation></semantics></math> in round 1 and reaches <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50.00\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50.00</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50.00\%</annotation></semantics></math> by round 50. Increasing the sample count raises accuracy at every round; for example, the first–round accuracy rises to <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24.81\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24.81</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24.81\%</annotation></semantics></math> and the round–50 accuracy reaches <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="72.53\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>72.53</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">72.53\%</annotation></semantics></math> when more training samples are available. The same upward trend holds for ISOLET and UCI-HAR: after 50 rounds, accuracy is <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="57.72\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>57.72</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">57.72\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62.74\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62.74</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62.74\%</annotation></semantics></math> with 50 samples per client, and increases to <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75.75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75.75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75.75\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="78.69\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>78.69</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">78.69\%</annotation></semantics></math> when each client has 500 samples. Across the tested hypervector sizes, the effect of adding training samples dominates, producing consistent gains under non-IID partitions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10691v1/mnist_non_D_acc.svg" id="S5.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/117;" width="238" height="117"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Accuracy of PrivateDFL over communication rounds for different hypervector dimensions (<math id="S5.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>) on the non-IID MNIST dataset.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, Figure <a href="#S5.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> examines the effect of hypervector dimensionality under non-IID partitions for MNIST. Accuracy at round 1 is close to <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> for all tested sizes. For a very small dimension such as <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=100</annotation></semantics></math>, the model lacks capacity to retain class information and the injected DP noise dominates, so accuracy decreases as rounds proceed; at rounds <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math>, the values are <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="46.78\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>46.78</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">46.78\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40.18\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40.18</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40.18\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36.76\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>36.76</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36.76\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35.10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35.10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35.10\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Increasing <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> counteracts this effect. With <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=500</annotation></semantics></math>, accuracy improves across rounds, reaching <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70.57\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70.57</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70.57\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="74.66\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>74.66</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">74.66\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="76.86\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>76.86</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">76.86\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="78.25\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>78.25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">78.25\%</annotation></semantics></math> at rounds <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="150" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>150</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">150</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math>. Larger dimensions provide further gains: after <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, accuracy is <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86.93\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>86.93</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86.93\%</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=1000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90.95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90.95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90.95\%</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=3000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=3000</annotation></semantics></math>, which are increases of <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.68\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.68</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.68\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.70\%</annotation></semantics></math> relative to <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=500</annotation></semantics></math> at the same round. Beyond this range, returns diminish because larger <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> also increases the effective noise scale; for <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=5000</annotation></semantics></math>, the round <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> accuracy is <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90.94\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90.94</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90.94\%</annotation></semantics></math>, essentially the same as for <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=3000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=3000</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F10.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10691v1/mnist_bench_acc.svg" id="S5.F10.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:157/126;" width="157" height="126"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F10.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10691v1/isolet_bench_acc.svg" id="S5.F10.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:157/126;" width="157" height="126"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F10.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10691v1/ucihar_bench_acc.svg" id="S5.F10.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:157/126;" width="157" height="126"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Comparison of accuracy between PrivateDFL and baseline ML models with DP across datasets. (a) MNIST, (b) ISOLET, and (c) UCI-HAR.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Benchmark</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> compares PrivateDFL with differentially private ML baselines across MNIST, ISOLET, and UCI-HAR under IID and non-IID partitions. Across datasets, PrivateDFL consistently delivers the strongest accuracy while meeting the same privacy budgets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For MNIST (Figure <a href="#S5.F10.sf1" title="In Figure 10 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10(a)</span></a>), PrivateDFL attains <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95.74\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95.74</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95.74\%</annotation></semantics></math> in IID and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="89.38\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>89.38</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">89.38\%</annotation></semantics></math> in non-IID. DP regularized deep models lag well behind. AlexNet reaches <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.27\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11.27</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.27\%</annotation></semantics></math> with DP-SGD and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.51\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.51</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.51\%</annotation></semantics></math> with RDP, GoogLeNet achieves <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="52.60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>52.60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">52.60\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="46.53\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>46.53</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">46.53\%</annotation></semantics></math>, ResNet50 reaches <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="79.59\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>79.59</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">79.59\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.83\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>77.83</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.83\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and Vision Transformer reaches <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64.96\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64.96</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64.96\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64.47\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64.47</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64.47\%</annotation></semantics></math>. These outcomes indicate that the hyperdimensional backbone of PrivateDFL preserves utility more effectively under privacy noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For ISOLET (Figure <a href="#S5.F10.sf2" title="In Figure 10 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10(b)</span></a>), PrivateDFL leads with <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="88.45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>88.45</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">88.45\%</annotation></semantics></math> in IID and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86.66\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>86.66</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86.66\%</annotation></semantics></math> in non-IID. Transformer and CNN baselines trained with DP remain between <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.78\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.78</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.78\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.26\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.26</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.26\%</annotation></semantics></math>. RNN yields <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25.59\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25.59</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25.59\%</annotation></semantics></math> with DP-SGD and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.09\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>23.09</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.09\%</annotation></semantics></math> with RDP, while TCN stays below <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\%</annotation></semantics></math>. The pronounced gap shows that sequential deep models are highly sensitive to DP noise, whereas PrivateDFL sustains strong accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For UCI-HAR (Figure <a href="#S5.F10.sf3" title="In Figure 10 ‣ 5.1 Sensitivity Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Results ‣ Privacy-Preserving Decentralized Federated Learning via Explainable Adaptive Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10(c)</span></a>), PrivateDFL reaches <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94.30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94.30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94.30\%</annotation></semantics></math> in IID and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="92.33\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>92.33</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">92.33\%</annotation></semantics></math> in non-IID. Among tabular baselines, TabTransformer attains <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47.34\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47.34</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47.34\%</annotation></semantics></math> with DP-SGD and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="37.53\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>37.53</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">37.53\%</annotation></semantics></math> with RDP, TabNet yields <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="37.73\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>37.73</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">37.73\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="37.36\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>37.36</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">37.36\%</annotation></semantics></math>, DNN reaches <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="79.47\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>79.47</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">79.47\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="78.72\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>78.72</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">78.72\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and DCN is near <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18.87\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18.87</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18.87\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18.83\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18.83</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18.83\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Taken together, these results show that PrivateDFL balances privacy and utility more effectively than conventional ML with DP across image, speech, and sensor modalities.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F11" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
